<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wiryeseong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wiryeseong was the first capital of Baekje, said in tradition to have been founded by Onjo in 18 BCE on the Han river. It is usually identified with the earthen fortresses of Pungnap and Mongchon in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, which later formed part of the capital known as Hanseong, captured by Goguryeo in 475.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wiryeseong.jpg" alt="Wiryeseong" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Straitgate · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pungnap_Toseong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wiryeseong was the name of two early capitals of Baekje, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea">Three Kingdoms of Korea</a>, both believed to have stood in the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. The earlier one is called Habuk Wiryeseong, north of the river, and the later one Hanam Wiryeseong, south of the river. Many scholars take the earthen walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pungnap-toseong">Pungnap Toseong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongchon-toseong">Mongchon Toseong</a> in Songpa District, Seoul, to be the remains of Hanam Wiryeseong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The account of the founding comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>, the oldest surviving Korean history, written in the 12th century. In it Onjo, a son of Goguryeo's founder Jumong, founded the state of Sipje, later Baekje, at Wiryeseong, while his elder brother Biryu settled at Michuhol further west, usually placed at present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a>. Finding the land of Michuhol too barren and saline to support his people, Biryu moved them to Wiryeseong, and shortly afterwards the state's name changed from Sipje to Baekje. Onjo later moved the capital further south because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Malgal</a> to the north and Lelang to the east.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a> period Baekje grew in conflict with southern Mahan and with the Chinese commanderies to the north, among them Daifang. It reshaped its political system, extended its territory into Mahan and the Hwanghae Province region, and became a regional power. Wiryeseong remained the capital until 475, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">King Jangsu</a> of Goguryeo captured it together with the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> area and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaero-of-baekje">King Gaero</a> of Baekje. Gaero's successor, King Munju, moved south and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Ungjin</a>, modern Gongju, the new capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wiryeseong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Korean tradition, preserved in the Samguk sagi, holds that Onjo, a son of Jumong, the founder of Goguryeo, came south with followers and founded the kingdom in 18 BCE at <b>Wiryeseong</b>; another version credits his elder brother Biryu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
